I kept the ranking step separate from the replacement step. First, sort the distinct values. Their positions in that sorted list give the rank, starting from 1. A dictionary then lets us replace every original value without changing the order of the input.

Dry run: for `[40, 10, 20, 10]`, the sorted distinct values are `[10, 20, 40]`. Their ranks are `10 -> 1`, `20 -> 2`, and `40 -> 3`, so the result is `[3, 1, 2, 1]`.

```python
class Solution:
    def replace_with_rank(self, arr):
        if not arr:
            return []

        sorted_values = sorted(set(arr))
        rank = {value: index + 1 for index, value in enumerate(sorted_values)}

        return [rank[value] for value in arr]
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the sorted values and dictionary.

This version makes a sorted copy of the input and uses it to build a value-to-rank table. I skip repeated values while assigning ranks, so equal elements always receive the same number.

For example, `[5, 5, 2, 9]` becomes `[2, 2, 1, 3]`. Sorting the copy does not affect the original list.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> replaceWithRank(List<Integer> arr) {
        if (arr == null || arr.isEmpty()) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        }

        List<Integer> sortedValues = new ArrayList<>(arr);
        Collections.sort(sortedValues);

        Map<Integer, Integer> ranks = new HashMap<>();
        int nextRank = 1;
        for (int value : sortedValues) {
            if (!ranks.containsKey(value)) {
                ranks.put(value, nextRank++);
            }
        }

        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>(arr.size());
        for (int value : arr) {
            result.add(ranks.get(value));
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because of the sort.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the copied list and map.

An ordered set removes duplicates and keeps the values in increasing order. I assign ranks while walking through that set, then look up each original value in a hash map.

The input array is not changed. The empty array is also handled before reading its values.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> replaceWithRank(vector<int>& arr) {
        if (arr.empty()) {
            return {};
        }

        set<int> uniqueValues(arr.begin(), arr.end());
        unordered_map<int, int> rank;

        int currentRank = 1;
        for (int value : uniqueValues) {
            rank[value] = currentRank++;
        }

        vector<int> result;
        result.reserve(arr.size());
        for (int value : arr) {
            result.push_back(rank[value]);
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

The set construction takes `O(n log n)`, and the remaining passes take expected `O(n)`. Overall time is `O(n log n)`, with `O(n)` extra space.

For each number, this checks how many different values are smaller than it. That count plus one is the rank. A small set records the smaller values so duplicates are not counted more than once.

This is easier to follow than sorting, but it is slower for large arrays. It also leaves the input unchanged. An array with one value gives rank 1 for every element.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> replaceWithRank(vector<int>& arr) {
        vector<int> result;
        result.reserve(arr.size());

        for (int value : arr) {
            set<int> smallerValues;
            for (int other : arr) {
                if (other < value) {
                    smallerValues.insert(other);
                }
            }
            result.push_back(static_cast<int>(smallerValues.size()) + 1);
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity is `O(n^2 log n)` because each element may insert many smaller values into a set. Space complexity is `O(n)` for the temporary set and result.

